<div id="2026-09-13-hdry-br01-naming-correction" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-09-13 — HDRY BR01 naming correction + generic part-version register</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> The "missing BR01 STL" was never missing: it is <code>BR03.stl</code> (150&nbsp;mm, the L1 short base post). The importer had pointed model 26 (BR01) at <code>BR02.stl</code> (215&nbsp;mm L2). Same fix for FL01/BL01/BBR01 &rarr; FL03/BL03/BBR03. Also shipped a generic, non-3D-specific part-version register under <code>tracking/</code>.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> Earlier audit claimed L1 parts were "never created" and proved <code>BR03 IS BR01</code> by mirror-geometry only — that detector can't tell a 150&nbsp;mm post from a 215&nbsp;mm one of the same footprint. Measured z-height (150 vs 215) is the reliable separator. This is the same provider version/naming-drift risk any BOM project faces: creator re-releases plates with new filenames, and a filename-only importer silently binds a BOM line to the wrong-height file.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>hdry_work/STL_EXTRACTION_AUDIT.md</code> (corrected), <code>tracking/generate_part_register.py</code>, <code>tracking/hdry_manifest.json</code>, <code>tracking/part_version_register.csv</code>. The CSV is geometry-measured and has version-rotation columns (<code>upstream_url</code>, <code>upstream_version</code>, <code>change_found</code>, <code>change_notes</code>, <code>action_needed</code>) usable in any spreadsheet for any project with BOMs.</p>
</div>
